    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAASn//gSM\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/common/listdirectives.py",
        "patchSetId": 33
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 4
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-09-04T00:47:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this is an interesting design choice, because i think it would be impossible for a driver to properly determine if it satisfied a filter if the parameters of the filter became more advanced over time in a backwards compatible fashion. only the caller can really know if the \"directives\" were satisfied as expected.\n\nfor example, say all filtering is case-sensitive in the first implementation. so, drivers happily mark \u0027satisfied\u0027 when a filter provides a case sensitive comparison.\n\nbut then, the optional directives thing evolves to support case insensitivity... the driver would continue to mark \u0027satisfied\u0027, which i suppose is true because it did some filtering but it\u0027s now returning a superset of the expected results. only the caller can look at the result set and see that filtering needs to be applied to properly satisfy the hints.",
      "revId": "379c934f377df972890faddf7a3a35577a7deca1",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAASn//ev8\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/common/listdirectives.py",
        "patchSetId": 33
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-09-04T10:31:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I think the driver should know if it can handle the filter, for instance whether the SQL it emits will be case sensitive or not (in fact, that is the case in code for the inexact filters today, even though they are disabled)",
      "parentUuid": "AAAASn//gSM\u003d",
      "revId": "379c934f377df972890faddf7a3a35577a7deca1",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
